THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
================================================================

The published package declares this distribution's own Windows Bash
platform package as an OPTIONAL dependency, and the same package — this
distribution's own tarball, carrying the pinned upstream runtime
unchanged — is attached to the GitHub Release for a tag. It is optional
in the dependency sense: npm fetches it automatically on Windows x64 and
skips it on platforms it does not cover.

  * `@dsh-ops/bash-win32-x64` — a declared optional dependency.
    This distribution carries the pinned upstream release unchanged: Git
    for Windows' `PortableGit-2.56.0.2-64-bit.7z.exe` (Git for Windows
    `v2.56.0.windows.2`, GNU bash 5.3.15). The pin records that upstream
    release, the official URL that publishes it, and its SHA-256; the
    assembly verifies the archive and the executable against the pin
    before publishing it. When the optional install is absent or
    incomplete, `dsh-ops bash-runtime install` reads the same pin,
    downloads that asset on the user's own machine, verifies the digest
    there, and unpacks it there. Git for Windows is distributed under the
    GNU General Public License version 2, so its bytes are GPL-2.0 and
    are used under that licence; its terms, its copyright, and the
    corresponding source belong to the Git for Windows project
    (https://github.com/git-for-windows/git) and to the Git project
    (https://github.com/git/git). Its original licences and notices are
    kept with the payload.

  * PowerShell remains managed by the host. `dsh-ops upgrade-host-pwsh`
    previews a standard Windows installation and `--yes` executes WinGet.
    It does not override the host executor or remove existing copies.
    The legacy provisioner retains a PIN, not a redistribution.
    This distribution does not contain, forward, repackage, or
    redistribute PowerShell. The pin records the upstream release
    (`PowerShell-7.6.6-win-x64.zip`, PowerShell `v7.6.6`), the official
    URL that publishes it, and its SHA-256; `dsh-ops provision-shells
    --pwsh` reads the pin, downloads that asset on the user's own
    machine, and verifies the digest there; OPS does not automatically
    activate that legacy copy. PowerShell is distributed by
    Microsoft under the MIT licence; that licence text, its copyright,
    and the corresponding source belong to Microsoft and to the
    PowerShell project (https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell).

